nd perhaps Old Norse toddi (“piece”). Also related with Old High German zata, zato (“flock”), Old Norse taða (“manured meadow”), from Proto-Germanic *tadǭ. Probably from the root Proto-Indo-European *deh₂- (“to split, cut, divide”). Lack of lengthening before -t- is regular in Upper German, so there is no need to assume ideophonic gemination (though this is possible). A Central German doublet is Zote (“dirty joke”), which see.",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[678848,"zotte","noun","a flock of hair or fur clotted by moisture, dirt etc.",0,"f"]]},"zulegend":{"entries":[["zulegend",null,null,null]],"senses":[[350772,"zulegend","verb","present participle of zulegen",0]],"inflections":[["zulegend","zulegen","participle,present"]]},"beihalfst":{"inflections":[["beihalfst","beihelfen","indicative,preterite,second-person,singular,subordinate-clause"]]},"fondsmanagementen":{"inflections":[["fondsmanagementen","fondsmanagement","dative,definite,plural"]]},"gefallsüchtigem":{"inflections":[["gefallsüchtigem","gefallsüchtig","dative,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"halsstarrigem":{"inflections":[["halsstarrigem","halsstarrig","dative,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"schlüssle":{"inflections":[["schlüssle","schlüsseln","first-person,indicative,present,singular"]]}}}